---
// Sitewide custom cursor — a single quiet off-white dot that becomes a thin
// hexagon outline (dot still in its centre) over anything interactive.
//
// Design intent (this is a dark cobalt/indigo "blue-hour terminal" site): the
// cursor must stay subtle. The DEFAULT is JUST a dot — NO ring, NO scanner
// sweep, NO text/labels, ever, anywhere. Not on hover, not at the bottom of the
// home page when the exploration HUD "activates" (`data-exploring="true"` on
// .bh-root + body.is-scrolled). The only shape change the cursor ever makes is
// dot → hexagon+dot over interactive elements.
//
// COMET TRAIL (the one motion the cursor has): the dot/ball IS the cursor — it
// stays pinned exactly on the pointer so the cursor is precise and LEADS. Behind
// it a soft luminous tail FOLLOWS THROUGH and CURVES along the path just travelled
// (bright head on the ball → tapering, fading to nothing at the tail), then
// catches up and vanishes the instant the cursor settles. At rest there is only
// the clean ball.
//
// FLUSH HEAD, SOFT TAIL: the comet's HEAD is locked to the RAW pointer (the exact
// point the ball sits on), so the beam never detaches from the ball — even mid
// fast-flick. Only the BODY/tail eases (POINT_EASE), so it softly follows through
// behind the locked head. The ball itself is written SYNCHRONOUSLY in the
// mousemove handler (not deferred to the next rAF), so there is zero one-frame lag.
//
// The tail is a canvas-2D *particle/point trail* (the standard cursor-trail
// technique, e.g. nayutalienx/cursor-trail): a ring buffer of recent eased points,
// each with a spawn time; on a fast move we INTERPOLATE extra points along the
// segment (so the tail follows the curve instead of being a stiff straight bar),
// every point fades by age, and the path is stroked ONCE — raw-pointer head →
// eased ring body → tail tip — as a rounded, quadratic-smoothed curve with a
// single linear-gradient taper (bright head, fading to nothing) and an additive
// glow. A single straight rotated rectangle (the old approach) could not bend.
//
// PERFORMANCE: the rAF loop IDLES when the cursor is still (mirrors the hero
// scene's `raf = 0` sentinel + visibilitychange restart) — zero CPU at rest. No
// per-frame style-property thrash, no CSS `filter: blur` on a moving element, and
// the point buffer is preallocated (no per-frame allocations).
//
// This is a self-contained component: scoped <style> + a guarded inline <script>.
// Keeping it cohesive in one file means it can be dropped into BaseLayout once
// and work sitewide without touching the CSS cascade (tokens.css etc.). The
// component renders nothing meaningful for SSR/no-JS — the markup is inert until
// the script wires it up, and the native cursor is only hidden once we've
// confirmed a fine pointer, so keyboard/touch users are never left cursorless.
---

{/* The comet-tail canvas — its OWN fixed full-viewport layer, BEHIND the ball
    (one z-index step below), so its pixels are raw viewport coords fully
    decoupled from the ball's transform. Inert + invisible until the script wires
    it up and the first fine-pointer move reveals it.

    transition:persist takes an EXPLICIT, STABLE key ("bh-cursor-trail"). Without
    one Astro derives the persist id from the element's ordinal among transition-
    scoped nodes, which DIFFERS between a `bare` page (home/about) and a reading
    page — so the ids mismatched, persist SILENTLY failed on those swaps, and Astro
    inserted the fresh (un-revealed, opacity:0) node in place of the live one. The
    controller script runs once and holds the old node, so it never re-revealed the
    replacement → the cursor "sometimes" went invisible after a page transition.
    A fixed key makes the id identical on every page, so the SAME node truly
    persists across every navigation. Must match the ball's key family below. */}
<canvas class="cursor-trail" data-cursor-trail transition:persist="bh-cursor-trail" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

{/*
  The cursor root (the ball). position:fixed + pointer-events:none so it floats
  above all content and can never eat a click. Hidden by default (opacity 0); the
  script reveals it only on a fine pointer once the mouse has actually moved. The
  dot and the hexagon both live inside, centred on the same point, so the swap
  keeps the dot perfectly put.

  transition:persist takes an EXPLICIT, STABLE key ("bh-cursor-root") for the same
  reason as the trail canvas above — see that note. The ordinal-derived id differed
  across bare vs reading pages, so the live ball got replaced by a fresh opacity:0
  node on those swaps (the invisible-cursor bug); a fixed key persists the SAME
  node everywhere.
*/}
<div class="cursor" data-custom-cursor transition:persist="bh-cursor-root" aria-hidden="true">
  {/* The hexagon outline — hidden until .cursor--interactive. Flat-top regular
      hexagon (pointy sides), ~40px across, 1px-ish off-white stroke, no fill,
      low opacity. Drawn as an inline SVG <polygon> so it scales crisply. */}
  <svg class="cursor__hex" width="40" height="40" viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true">
    <polygon
      points="10,2.68 30,2.68 40,20 30,37.32 10,37.32 0,20"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1"
      vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"
    />
  </svg>
  {/* The constant centre dot — the whole default cursor. Stays put through the
      hexagon swap. */}
  <span class="cursor__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
</div>

<style>
  /* off-white — matches the site's --fg token (oklch(0.95 0.006 252)). Hardcoded
     here because this overlay lives outside the normal cascade (it's a
     `cursor: none` replacement) and must render even before any token resolves. */
  .cursor {
    --cursor-fg: oklch(0.95 0.006 252);

    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    /* Centre the whole rig on (0,0); the script translates by the raw pointer
       coords, and this -50%/-50% puts the dot/hex centre exactly on the tip. */
    margin-left: -20px; /* half of the 40px hex box */
    margin-top: -20px;
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;

    z-index: 2147483647;       /* above everything, including the HUD overlays */
    pointer-events: none;      /* never intercept clicks / selection */
    will-change: transform;    /* promote to its own layer for cheap rAF moves */

    color: var(--cursor-fg);
    opacity: 0;                /* revealed by the script on first fine-pointer move */
  }

  /* The comet-tail canvas: a full-viewport fixed layer, one z-index step BELOW
     the ball so the tail paints behind it. pointer-events:none means the
     full-bleed canvas never eats a click on the fixed overlays beneath it. The
     CSS box is the viewport; the pixel buffer (width/height attrs) is owned by
     the script and sized to viewport × capped DPR. */
  .cursor-trail {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100vw;
    height: 100vh;
    z-index: 2147483646;       /* one below the ball → tail behind the dot */
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;                /* revealed with the ball on first move */
  }

  /* The default cursor: a single 5px filled dot at ~38% opacity. Centred in the
     40px box. */
  .cursor__dot {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    width: 5px;
    height: 5px;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--cursor-fg);
    opacity: 0.38;
  }

  /* The hexagon outline: hidden + slightly shrunk by default, eased up to full
     size + ~50% opacity over interactive elements. The dot is unaffected. */
  .cursor__hex {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    color: var(--cursor-fg);
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(0.6);
    transform-origin: center;
    /* Crafted-but-quiet swap with the site's confident ease-out curve. */
    transition:
      opacity 140ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
      transform 140ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
  }

  .cursor.cursor--interactive .cursor__hex {
    opacity: 0.5;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1);
  }

  /* DOCKED: the cursor has glided onto a LOCKED star-marker and stands in for the
     marker's gold inner hexagon. The hex turns GOLD and sizes to sit as a static
     inner border just inside the marker's white outer hex. It does NOT rotate or
     breathe — crisp and held still. The centre dot stays exactly as-is (still
     visible, still centred). The aged Voyager-gold below is the resolved value
     of the site's --highlight token (oklch(0.78 0.085 72)) — the same gold the
     marker's own inner hex used — hardcoded here because this overlay renders
     outside the token cascade (mirrors how --cursor-fg is hardcoded above).

     CAPTURE/RELEASE ANIMATION (driven entirely from JS each frame so it stays in
     lockstep with the position glide — see syncDock + loop): the script writes
       --dock-scale  the LIVE hex scale this frame (eases 0→target on capture, and
                     target→normal-interactive scale on release),
       --dock-gold   0..1 mix toward gold (1 = full gold, 0 = off-white),
       --dock-op     the LIVE hex opacity this frame (eases up on capture, down to
                     the normal interactive 0.5 on release).
     `transition: none` here so the per-frame JS writes are not fought by a CSS
     transition. color-mix blends off-white→gold by --dock-gold so the colour pop
     is gone: on capture it eases in with the scale, on release it eases back out
     together with the position+scale return (the simplest tasteful symmetry). */
  .cursor {
    --cursor-gold: oklch(0.78 0.085 72);
    --dock-scale: 0.95; /* live hex scale, written per-frame by the script */
    --dock-gold: 1;     /* live off-white→gold mix, written per-frame (1 = gold) */
    --dock-op: 0.9;     /* live hex opacity, written per-frame by the script */
  }
  .cursor.cursor--docked .cursor__hex {
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--cursor-fg), var(--cursor-gold) calc(var(--dock-gold) * 100%));
    opacity: var(--dock-op);
    /* No CSS transition — the script eases scale/opacity/colour every frame so
       the hex glides in on capture and back out on release without a snap. */
    transition: none;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(var(--dock-scale));
  }

  /* Reduced motion: keep the cursor fully functional but skip the scale/opacity
     animation — the hexagon snaps in/out instead of easing. The comet tail is
     also suppressed (the script never starts the draw loop under reduced motion,
     and this hides the canvas belt-and-braces). */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cursor__hex {
      transition: none;
    }
    .cursor-trail {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* Coarse / no-fine-pointer devices: never render the custom cursor at all.
     The script is also gated on (pointer: fine), but this is a belt-and-braces
     guard so the elements can never flash on a touch device before JS runs. */
  @media not (pointer: fine) {
    .cursor,
    .cursor-trail {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Custom-cursor controller. Runs once per page load (plain MPA Astro — each
  // navigation is a fresh document), guarded against double-init.
  //
  // Performance contract:
  //  - The BALL is pinned to the raw pointer with ONE translate3d, written
  //    SYNCHRONOUSLY in the mousemove handler (zero one-frame rAF lag). The loop
  //    re-writes the same idempotent transform for non-move wakes (reveal/resize).
  //  - The TAIL is drawn on a canvas from a preallocated ring buffer of recent
  //    eased points, with its HEAD locked to the raw pointer so it stays flush
  //    against the ball (no per-frame allocations, no style-property thrash, no
  //    CSS blur).
  //  - The rAF loop IDLES when the cursor settles (raf = 0; no frame scheduled)
  //    and restarts on the next move — zero CPU at rest. Mirrors the hero scene
  //    loop in src/hero/scene/createScene.ts (raf=0 sentinel + visibilitychange).
  (() => {
    // Only enable for a genuine fine pointer (mouse / trackpad). On touch /
    // coarse pointers we do nothing: no custom cursor AND we leave the native
    // cursor alone so nothing is ever left cursorless.
    if (!window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) return;

    const cursor = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-custom-cursor]');
    if (!cursor) return;

    // Guard against double-init (e.g. if the component were ever rendered twice
    // or the script re-evaluated). Mark the element once we've claimed it.
    if (cursor.dataset.cursorInit === 'true') return;
    cursor.dataset.cursorInit = 'true';

    const canvas = document.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('[data-cursor-trail]');
    const ctx = canvas ? canvas.getContext('2d') : null;

    // Hide the native cursor sitewide now that we know we'll draw our own. Done
    // in JS (not CSS) so non-fine-pointer / no-JS visitors keep their real
    // cursor. We set it on the root element so it cascades to everything.
    const hideStyle = document.createElement('style');
    hideStyle.textContent = `html, body, * { cursor: none !important; }`;
    document.head.appendChild(hideStyle);

    // RE-ARM AFTER EVERY SPA SWAP. BaseLayout mounts <ClientRouter />, and its
    // head diff REMOVES this dynamically-injected <style> on every SPA
    // navigation (it isn't part of any page's authored head). The cursor
    // elements themselves survive via transition:persist and this module script
    // is deduped (it never re-runs on a swap), so without this hook the native
    // cursor silently came back after the first SPA nav — the "cursor sometimes
    // switches back to the default" bug — while the custom dot kept tracking on
    // top of it. Re-appending the same node is idempotent and cheap.
    document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', () => {
      if (!hideStyle.isConnected) document.head.appendChild(hideStyle);
    });

    // Selector for things that should show the hexagon. `closest()` is used at
    // hit-test time so a child of a link/button (an icon, a <span>) still counts.
    const INTERACTIVE_SELECTOR = [
      'a[href]',
      'button',
      'input',
      'textarea',
      'select',
      'label',
      '[role="button"]',
      '[data-cursor="interactive"]',
    ].join(',');

    // Honour reduced-motion: with it on, the ball still tracks the pointer exactly
    // (written directly in onMove) but the comet tail / draw loop never runs.
    const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    // --- inlined house helpers (mirror src/hero, kept here so the IIFE stays
    //     self-contained — see lifecycle.ts easeOut + config.ts tuneRenderPixelRatio) ---
    // easeOutCubic: fast pull then soft settle — used to taper the tail head→tail.
    const easeOut = (x: number): number => 1 - Math.pow(1 - x, 3);
    // Cap the device pixel ratio so the offscreen buffer never gets huge on hiDPI.
    const cappedDpr = (): number => {
      const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
      const w = window.innerWidth;
      if (w < 1280) return Math.min(dpr, 1.6);
      return Math.min(dpr, 1.85);
    };

    // ---- tuning (a visible luminous comet on a dark cobalt bg — bright head,
    //      long soft fading tail, matching the reference image) ----
    // The DOT is pinned to the exact pointer synchronously in onMove (zero lag) and
    // the comet HEAD is locked to that same raw pointer in drawTrail. POINT_EASE is
    // only the TAIL BODY's lag: higher = the tail keeps up closer to the head so it
    // reads as one continuous comet rather than a detached lagging blob.
    const POINT_EASE = 0.35;       // tail anchor follows the (exact) dot snappily
    const SPAWN_STEP = 2.5;        // px between stored points — SMALLER = denser points = smoother curve
    const TRAIL_LIFE_MS = 260;     // tail length in TIME — SHORTER so the points pack densely (smoother)
    const TRAIL_WIDTH_MAX = 2.2;   // px — a THIN soft beam (matches the reference)
    const TRAIL_ALPHA_MAX = 0.5;   // head of the beam; additive over the dark bg
    const REST_EPS_SQ = 0.04;      // px²; eased anchor "caught the ball" threshold
    const CAP = 160;               // ring capacity (short life × dense spawn fits easily)
    const SUBDIV = 10;             // Catmull-Rom interpolated points per segment (curve smoothness)
    const TRAIL_RGB = '241, 242, 245'; // ≈ --fg oklch(0.95 0.006 252) in sRGB

    // Raw pointer (the ball is pinned here). The tail anchor (easeX/Y) lags it.
    let targetX = window.innerWidth / 2;
    let targetY = window.innerHeight / 2;
    let easeX = targetX;
    let easeY = targetY;

    // Ring buffer of recent points: parallel typed arrays → zero per-frame alloc.
    const px = new Float32Array(CAP); // x in CSS px
    const py = new Float32Array(CAP); // y in CSS px
    const pt = new Float32Array(CAP); // spawn timestamp (ms)
    let head = 0;   // index of the newest point
    let count = 0;  // number of live points (0..CAP)
    // Reuse buffers: the comet path unrolled head→tail each frame (no per-frame
    // alloc). lp[0] is the RAW-pointer head (prepended each frame); lp[1..] are the
    // eased ring points, so the head joins the smoothing as a normal point — that's
    // CAP ring points + 1 head = CAP + 1 slots.
    const lpx = new Float32Array(CAP + 1);
    const lpy = new Float32Array(CAP + 1);

    let revealed = false; // becomes visible on the first real move
    let raf = 0;          // rAF id; 0 = the loop is NOT running (idle)
    let viewW = window.innerWidth;
    let viewH = window.innerHeight;

    // ---- star-marker DOCK state (handshake with StarMarker.tsx) ----------------
    // When a star-marker LOCKS it publishes window.__bhMarkerLock = { active, x, y,
    // hexRadius }. While active the cursor DOCKS: instead of tracking the pointer it
    // GLIDES onto (x,y) and the gold hex SCALES IN, then HOLDS still as the marker's
    // gold inner hexagon (static, no trail). On release it GLIDES symmetrically back
    // toward the live pointer (gold + scale ease back out), then resumes normal
    // tracking. We flag <html data-cursor-docked> for the whole capture+hold+release
    // so CSS hides the marker's OWN gold hex (exactly one gold hex on screen — the
    // cursor) until the release glide fully completes. Cleared on undock.
    //
    // The animation is an exponential lerp on the existing rAF loop (no time-based
    // tweens, no per-frame allocations). renderX/Y is the position actually drawn
    // this frame; it eases toward the marker centre while capturing. RELEASE is
    // instant (clearDock the frame the lock drops), so there is no release glide.
    // dockAnim is the 0..1 capture envelope (0 = fully released, 1 = fully docked)
    // that drives the gold-mix, hex opacity and scale-in.
    let docked = false;     // a marker lock is currently engaged (capture or hold)
    let dockX = 0;          // marker centre to glide onto (CSS px)
    let dockY = 0;
    let renderX = targetX;  // position drawn this frame (eased; not the raw pointer)
    let renderY = targetY;
    let dockAnim = 0;       // 0..1 capture envelope: drives gold/opacity/scale
    let dockTargetScale = 0.95; // docked hex scale for the active lock (dockScaleFor)
    let pointerInside = true; // false while the pointer is outside the window
    // The marker's white outer hex polygon ≈ hexRadius * 2 * (100/116) px wide
    // (viewBox -8..108). The gold inner hex should sit at ~0.66× of that (a touch
    // above the marker's own ~0.56–0.62 breathe so the docked hex reads slightly
    // larger, but still well inside the white outer hex with a clear gap), and the
    // cursor's hex box is 40px wide, so:
    //   dockScale = (hexRadius * 2 * 100/116 * 0.66) / 40
    const HEX_VIEWBOX_RATIO = 100 / 116; // polygon span / viewBox span
    const INNER_HEX_FRACTION = 0.66;     // gold inner hex vs white outer hex
    const dockScaleFor = (hexRadius: number): number =>
      (hexRadius * 2 * HEX_VIEWBOX_RATIO * INNER_HEX_FRACTION) / 40;

    // ---- dock capture/release animation tuning -------------------------------
    // Per-frame exponential lerp factors (matches the file's POINT_EASE tail-anchor
    // approach). At ~60fps a factor f closes a fraction f of the remaining gap each
    // frame, settling to within ~2% in roughly -ln(0.02)/f frames ≈ (4/f)/60 s, so
    // 0.20 ≈ 20 frames ≈ 0.33s of glide with most of the visible travel in the first
    // ~220ms (a confident ease-out, mirroring cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)). POS
    // eases the cursor position (the glide), ANIM eases the 0..1 gold/opacity/scale
    // envelope (the scale-in). POS is kept a touch FASTER than ANIM so on release the
    // cursor reaches the pointer slightly before the gold finishes fading — the hex
    // is never left visibly drifting off-white (the gold IS the moving thing).
    const DOCK_POS_EASE = 0.2;    // position glide toward marker centre / pointer
    const DOCK_ANIM_EASE = 0.17;  // gold + opacity + scale-in envelope
    // The position ease above is asymptotic — it APPROACHES the marker centre but
    // never arrives, and the rAF loop idles once settled, so the docked hex used to
    // park several px short (measured ~6px down-left; see dockTransform's root-cause
    // note). These two close the tail so the dock is PIXEL-EXACT at any frame cadence
    // without changing the visible glide:
    //   • DOCK_POS_MIN_STEP — a floor on how far the position moves toward the target
    //     each frame, so the asymptote can't crawl to a halt while still off-centre.
    //   • DOCK_SNAP_EPS — once within this distance, snap EXACTLY onto the centre.
    //     Kept < 1px (under the gold hex's ~1.5px stroke) so the snap is invisible;
    //     only the imperceptible last sub-pixel is snapped, the glide stays eased.
    // A px/frame FLOOR on the closing glide. The eased fraction (dist * DOCK_POS_EASE)
    // dominates the visible travel while the cursor is still far from the centre; this
    // floor only takes over for the LAST few px, where the asymptote would otherwise
    // crawl (and stall outright if the rAF loop is being throttled while otherwise
    // idle). At 2.5px/frame the tail closes in ~2-3 frames even when frames are sparse,
    // so the dock is pixel-exact at any cadence; the value is small enough that the
    // close-in is imperceptible against the eased approach.
    const DOCK_POS_MIN_STEP = 2.5;  // px/frame floor on the closing glide
    const DOCK_SNAP_EPS = 0.6;      // px; within this, land exactly on centre
    // The gold hex starts the capture SMALL (scales up to dockTargetScale) — this
    // is the visible "hex forming into place" beat.
    const DOCK_SCALE_START = 0.5; // fraction of the docked scale the hex starts at
    const DOCK_HEX_OP = 0.9;      // hex opacity at full dock (matches old behaviour)
    const INTERACTIVE_HEX_OP = 0.5; // normal interactive hex opacity (release floor)

    // Write the docked hex CSS vars for the current envelope (anim 0..1) + scale.
    // anim drives: scale-in (DOCK_SCALE_START→1 of the docked scale), off-white→gold
    // mix, and hex opacity (interactive floor→full dock). One CSS-var batch; called
    // once per frame while docked / capturing. No allocations.
    const writeDockVars = (anim: number, scale: number): void => {
      const liveScale = scale * (DOCK_SCALE_START + (1 - DOCK_SCALE_START) * anim);
      const liveOp = INTERACTIVE_HEX_OP + (DOCK_HEX_OP - INTERACTIVE_HEX_OP) * anim;
      cursor.style.setProperty('--dock-scale', String(liveScale));
      cursor.style.setProperty('--dock-gold', String(anim));
      cursor.style.setProperty('--dock-op', String(liveOp));
    };

    // Eased step that GUARANTEES convergence: move `from` toward `to` by the larger
    // of the eased fraction and DOCK_POS_MIN_STEP, then SNAP exactly to `to` within
    // DOCK_SNAP_EPS (or when the floor step would overshoot). This keeps the visible
    // glide eased while killing the asymptotic shortfall that left the docked hex
    // parked off-centre when the rAF loop idled. Pure (no side effects); returns the
    // new coordinate this frame.
    const closeToward = (from: number, to: number): number => {
      const gap = to - from;
      const dist = Math.abs(gap);
      if (dist <= DOCK_SNAP_EPS) return to; // last sub-pixel: land exactly on centre
      const eased = dist * DOCK_POS_EASE;
      const step = Math.max(eased, DOCK_POS_MIN_STEP);
      if (step >= dist) return to;          // floor step would reach/overshoot → snap
      return from + Math.sign(gap) * step;
    };

    // ---- docked rig centring -------------------------------------------------
    // The docked gold hex sat visibly DOWN-LEFT of the marker's white outer hex.
    // EMPIRICAL ROOT CAUSE (pixel measurement at DPR 1 AND 2 — see
    // scratchpad/measure-hex-center.mjs): the gold-hex centre landed a CONSTANT ~6
    // CSS px up-and-left of the marker centre — and it was ~6px at BOTH 1× and 2×,
    // i.e. NOT scaling with device pixels, so it was never a half-device-pixel paint
    // bias. It was the POSITION EASE: `renderX += (dockX - renderX) * DOCK_POS_EASE`
    // is an asymptotic lerp that only ever APPROACHES dockX, and the rAF loop idles
    // once the cursor settles — so the glide PARKED several px short of the marker
    // centre (the gap left when the loop stopped scheduling frames). The white hex is
    // a separate element painted at the true marker centre, so that shortfall read as
    // the gold hex off-centre inside the white one.
    //
    // FIX: the glide stays eased (the visible travel is unchanged), but its TAIL is
    // closed exactly — see the dock branch in loop(): each frame the position also
    // moves at least DOCK_POS_MIN_STEP px toward the target, and within DOCK_SNAP_EPS
    // it SNAPS onto the centre. DOCK_SNAP_EPS is < 1px (under the gold hex's ~1.5px
    // stroke) so the snap is invisible, yet the settled hex is now pixel-exact at any
    // frame cadence. The transform therefore writes renderX/renderY directly — no
    // extra correction term (the disproven +0.5-device-px nudge is gone).
    const dockTransform = (x: number, y: number): string =>
      `translate3d(${x}px, ${y}px, 0)`;

    // Push a point into the ring (overwriting the oldest once full).
    const pushPoint = (x: number, y: number, now: number): void => {
      head = (head + 1) % CAP;
      px[head] = x;
      py[head] = y;
      pt[head] = now;
      if (count < CAP) count++;
    };

    // Size the canvas backing store to viewport × capped DPR and set the
    // transform so we can draw in CSS-px coordinates thereafter.
    const resizeCanvas = (): void => {
      if (!canvas || !ctx) return;
      const dpr = cappedDpr();
      viewW = window.innerWidth;
      viewH = window.innerHeight;
      canvas.width = Math.round(viewW * dpr);
      canvas.height = Math.round(viewH * dpr);
      canvas.style.width = viewW + 'px';
      canvas.style.height = viewH + 'px';
      ctx.setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0); // reset before re-scaling (idempotent)
      ctx.scale(dpr, dpr);
    };

    // Draw the comet from the ring buffer. Returns true if anything was drawn
    // (i.e. the tail is still alive), false once it has fully decayed.
    //
    // FLUSH HEAD, SOFT TAIL: the comet HEAD is the RAW pointer (headX, headY) —
    // the exact same point the ball is pinned to — NOT the eased ring anchor. So
    // the beam ALWAYS starts flush against the ball with zero gap, even mid-flick.
    // Behind that locked head, the eased ring points (lpx/lpy, lagged by
    // POINT_EASE) form the curving BODY/tail that follows through. We stitch the
    // raw head to the ring with one continuous path so it's one comet, not a head
    // detached from a lagging blob.
    //
    // SMOOTHNESS: the tail is ONE continuous path stroked ONCE. The points are
    // INTERPOLATED with a Catmull-Rom spline (SUBDIV extra points per segment) so
    // the curve is smooth even when the stored points are sparse — no facets, no
    // angular kinks, including at the head. The old per-segment strokes (each with
    // its own alpha, additively blended) overlapped unevenly on fast moves — bright
    // where caps overlapped, dim where they barely met — which read as a DASHED
    // line; a single stroke has no internal seams. The head→tail fade comes from a
    // GRADIENT strokeStyle along the path, not per-segment alpha, so it's a clean
    // continuous comet.
    const drawTrail = (now: number, headX: number, headY: number): boolean => {
      if (!ctx) return false;
      ctx.clearRect(0, 0, viewW, viewH);
      // The raw pointer is always the head, so we only need ONE live ring point to
      // draw a beam (head→ring). A single fast flick (count===1) still renders.
      if (count < 1) return false;

      // Build the comet polyline into the reuse buffer with the RAW pointer as the
      // FIRST point (lp[0]), then the live ring points (newest → oldest), stopping
      // at the first dead one. Because the head is just point 0 of the sequence, it
      // flows through the SAME smoothing as every other point — there is no
      // special-cased straight head segment to kink (the old `moveTo(head)` then
      // `quadraticCurveTo(ring0, …)` made a visible elbow at the ball).
      lpx[0] = headX;
      lpy[0] = headY;
      let live = 1;
      for (let n = 0; n < count; n++) {
        const i = (head - n + CAP) % CAP;
        if (1 - (now - pt[i]) / TRAIL_LIFE_MS <= 0) break;
        lpx[live] = px[i];
        lpy[live] = py[i];
        live++;
      }
      if (live < 2) return false;                     // need head + ≥1 ring point

      const hx = lpx[0], hy = lpy[0];                 // head: the RAW pointer (= ball)
      const tx = lpx[live - 1], ty = lpy[live - 1];   // tail tip (oldest live ring point)

      ctx.lineCap = 'round';
      ctx.lineJoin = 'round';
      ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter';       // additive glow on the dark bg
      ctx.globalAlpha = 1;

      // Build ONE smooth path through the whole polyline (head + ring), stroked a
      // SINGLE time. We INTERPOLATE the curve with a centripetal-style Catmull-Rom
      // spline: SUBDIV extra points per segment, so even sparse stored points (a
      // slow drift only stamps one point per several frames) render as a continuous
      // curve with no facets. Catmull-Rom passes THROUGH every control point, so the
      // beam still starts exactly on the ball (lp[0] = raw head) and ends exactly at
      // the tail tip. Endpoints are clamped (the boundary point is duplicated as the
      // phantom neighbour) so the spline is well-defined at the head and tail.
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(hx, hy);
      if (live === 2) {
        // Just head + one ring point: a single straight span (nothing to smooth).
        ctx.lineTo(tx, ty);
      } else {
        for (let n = 0; n < live - 1; n++) {
          // Control points for the segment lp[n] → lp[n+1], with clamped phantoms.
          const p0x = lpx[n === 0 ? 0 : n - 1], p0y = lpy[n === 0 ? 0 : n - 1];
          const p1x = lpx[n],                   p1y = lpy[n];
          const p2x = lpx[n + 1],               p2y = lpy[n + 1];
          const i3 = n + 2 < live ? n + 2 : live - 1;
          const p3x = lpx[i3],                  p3y = lpy[i3];
          // Emit SUBDIV interpolated points across this segment (t in (0,1]).
          for (let s = 1; s <= SUBDIV; s++) {
            const t = s / SUBDIV;
            const t2 = t * t;
            const t3 = t2 * t;
            // Catmull-Rom basis (uniform, 0.5 tension) → a point ON the curve.
            const cx =
              0.5 * (2 * p1x + (-p0x + p2x) * t +
                (2 * p0x - 5 * p1x + 4 * p2x - p3x) * t2 +
                (-p0x + 3 * p1x - 3 * p2x + p3x) * t3);
            const cy =
              0.5 * (2 * p1y + (-p0y + p2y) * t +
                (2 * p0y - 5 * p1y + 4 * p2y - p3y) * t2 +
                (-p0y + 3 * p1y - 3 * p2y + p3y) * t3);
            ctx.lineTo(cx, cy);
          }
        }
      }

      // Gradient along the head→tail axis: bright at the head, fading to nothing
      // at the tail. This is the comet taper, done as one continuous fill so there
      // are no per-segment seams (no dashing).
      // Gradient fades the beam from its head down to nothing at the tail. The
      // head stops just SHORT of full strength and the dot itself is a separate
      // opaque CSS element — so the DOT never lights up / haloes (per the
      // reference: a clean solid ball with only a thin beam behind it). There is
      // deliberately NO radial glow around the head.
      const grad = ctx.createLinearGradient(hx, hy, tx, ty);
      grad.addColorStop(0, `rgba(${TRAIL_RGB}, ${TRAIL_ALPHA_MAX})`);
      grad.addColorStop(0.35, `rgba(${TRAIL_RGB}, ${TRAIL_ALPHA_MAX * 0.5})`);
      grad.addColorStop(1, `rgba(${TRAIL_RGB}, 0)`);
      ctx.strokeStyle = grad;
      ctx.lineWidth = TRAIL_WIDTH_MAX;
      ctx.stroke();

      ctx.globalAlpha = 1;                            // restore for cleanliness
      ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';
      return true;
    };

    // Fully clear the dock (class, html attr, trail) and resume normal pointer
    // tracking. Snaps the tail anchor + render position onto the live pointer and
    // clears the ring so the trail re-grows cleanly FROM the pointer (no streak
    // from the old dock position). Called the instant the lock drops (release is
    // instant in both motion modes — no glide-back).
    const clearDock = (): void => {
      docked = false;
      dockAnim = 0;
      cursor.classList.remove('cursor--docked');
      delete document.documentElement.dataset.cursorDocked;
      renderX = targetX;
      renderY = targetY;
      easeX = targetX;
      easeY = targetY;
      head = 0;
      count = 0;
      if (ctx) ctx.clearRect(0, 0, viewW, viewH);
    };

    // Read window.__bhMarkerLock and reconcile the dock LIFECYCLE (the class, the
    // html attr, dock coords, the docked scale, and the docked flag). Release is
    // instant: clearDock() fires the frame the lock drops, then this returns false.
    // Returns true while the loop should run its dock branch (capturing / holding)
    // — the actual position glide + scale-in easing happens in loop.
    // Only touches DOM on a state edge, so it's cheap to call every frame. Mirrors
    // how onMove reads window.__bhHitGiant.
    type MarkerLock = { active: boolean; x: number; y: number; hexRadius: number };
    const syncDock = (): boolean => {
      const lock = (window as unknown as { __bhMarkerLock?: MarkerLock | null })
        .__bhMarkerLock;
      // Only dock while the pointer is actually inside the window (a hidden cursor
      // must not keep the marker's gold hex suppressed).
      const active = pointerInside && !!lock && lock.active === true;

      if (active && lock) {
        // Capture (or hold): follow the live marker centre + remember its scale.
        dockX = lock.x;
        dockY = lock.y;
        dockTargetScale = dockScaleFor(lock.hexRadius);
        if (!docked) {
          docked = true;
          // Turn gold + suppress the marker's own hex from the very first frame so
          // the dock reads as the hex FORMING into place (gold present, scaling in).
          cursor.classList.add('cursor--docked');
          document.documentElement.dataset.cursorDocked = 'true';
          if (reduceMotion) {
            // Reduced motion: no glide / scale-in — snap straight to full dock.
            dockAnim = 1;
            renderX = dockX;
            renderY = dockY;
            writeDockVars(1, dockTargetScale);
          } else {
            // Fresh capture: seed the glide START at the live pointer (release is
            // instant, so every dock is a fresh capture) and the envelope at 0 so the
            // gold hex scales IN from small.
            renderX = targetX;
            renderY = targetY;
            dockAnim = 0;
          }
        }
        return true;
      }

      // Not active. Release is INSTANT (no glide-back): the moment the lock drops,
      // snap straight back to following the raw pointer. The capture is animated, but
      // the way OUT must have zero friction — a return glide felt sticky/laggy. So we
      // clearDock() immediately in BOTH motion modes; the cursor resumes normal
      // pointer pinning + comet trail this very frame.
      if (docked) {
        clearDock();
      }
      return false;
    };

    const loop = (): void => {
      // Pause entirely when the tab is hidden (mirrors the hero scene loop).
      if (document.hidden) { raf = 0; return; }
      const now = performance.now();

      // DOCK branch: capturing or holding a star-marker lock. Instead of snapping IN,
      // GLIDE: ease renderX/Y toward the marker centre and ease the 0..1 envelope
      // (gold + opacity + scale-in) up to 1. Draw NO trail, and keep the loop alive
      // (the marker may release without a mousemove — scroll / focus). RELEASE is
      // instant: syncDock() calls clearDock() the frame the lock drops and returns
      // false, so we fall straight through to normal raw-pointer pinning with no
      // glide-back. One transform write + one CSS-var batch per frame; no allocs.
      if (syncDock()) {
        // Eased glide toward the marker centre, but with the asymptotic TAIL closed
        // (min step + snap) so the hex lands pixel-exact rather than parking ~6px
        // short when the loop idles — see DOCK_POS_MIN_STEP / DOCK_SNAP_EPS.
        renderX = closeToward(renderX, dockX);
        renderY = closeToward(renderY, dockY);
        dockAnim += (1 - dockAnim) * DOCK_ANIM_EASE;
        cursor.style.transform = dockTransform(renderX, renderY);
        writeDockVars(dockAnim, dockTargetScale);
        raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
        return;
      }

      // The ball: pinned to the raw pointer (no easing) so the cursor is exact.
      // onMove already wrote this transform synchronously on the mousemove event
      // (zero rAF lag — the "flush head" half of the fix), so this re-write is
      // idempotent: targetX/Y don't change between moves, so it paints the same
      // pixel. It's kept so the FIRST reveal frame and any loop-only wake (resize /
      // visibility / mouseleave clear) still position the ball without a mousemove.
      cursor.style.transform = `translate3d(${targetX}px, ${targetY}px, 0)`;

      // Ease the tail anchor toward the ball; spawn points along the path it
      // covers this frame so a fast move lays a dense, curving trail (not one
      // far-apart pair). This interpolation is what lets the tail BEND.
      const lastX = count ? px[head] : easeX;
      const lastY = count ? py[head] : easeY;
      easeX += (targetX - easeX) * POINT_EASE;
      easeY += (targetY - easeY) * POINT_EASE;

      const dx = easeX - lastX;
      const dy = easeY - lastY;
      const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
      if (count === 0) {
        pushPoint(easeX, easeY, now);
      } else if (dist >= SPAWN_STEP) {
        // Walk the segment in SPAWN_STEP increments, stamping a point each step.
        const steps = Math.min(CAP, Math.floor(dist / SPAWN_STEP));
        const ux = dx / dist;
        const uy = dy / dist;
        for (let s = 1; s <= steps; s++) {
          const d = s * SPAWN_STEP;
          pushPoint(lastX + ux * d, lastY + uy * d, now);
        }
      }

      const alive = drawTrail(now, targetX, targetY);

      // Idle test: the eased anchor has caught the ball AND nothing is left to
      // draw → reset and STOP the loop (no frame scheduled). Re-armed on move.
      const caughtUp = (targetX - easeX) ** 2 + (targetY - easeY) ** 2 < REST_EPS_SQ;
      if (caughtUp && !alive) {
        head = 0;
        count = 0;
        if (ctx) ctx.clearRect(0, 0, viewW, viewH);
        raf = 0;
        return;
      }
      raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
    };

    // (Re)start the draw loop only if it isn't already running (and we animate).
    const kick = (): void => {
      if (raf === 0 && !reduceMotion && !document.hidden) {
        raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
      }
    };

    const onMove = (event: MouseEvent): void => {
      targetX = event.clientX;
      targetY = event.clientY;
      pointerInside = true;

      // First real movement: reveal the cursor (avoids a stray dot parked in the
      // top-left before the pointer has been located). Snap the anchor onto the
      // ball and empty the ring so no tail streaks in from the screen centre.
      if (!revealed) {
        revealed = true;
        easeX = targetX;
        easeY = targetY;
        head = 0;
        count = 0;
        cursor.style.opacity = '1';
        if (canvas) canvas.style.opacity = '1';
      }

      // DOCKED (or capturing) a locked star-marker: the loop owns the glide +
      // scale-in, so skip the interactive hit-test + trail. Under reduced motion the
      // loop never runs, so write the snapped dock transform here too; otherwise just
      // (re)start the loop so it glides + watches for the (instant) undock. If the
      // lock just dropped, syncDock() already cleared the dock and returns false, so
      // we fall through to normal pointer handling this frame (no glide-back).
      if (syncDock()) {
        if (reduceMotion) {
          // No glide under reduced motion: snap straight onto the marker centre
          // (dockX/dockY ARE the centre — dockTransform applies no correction term).
          cursor.style.transform = dockTransform(dockX, dockY);
        } else {
          kick();
        }
        return;
      }

      // Interactive hit-test. The target may be a child of the link/button, so
      // walk up with closest(); also honour any element whose *computed* cursor
      // is `pointer` (covers elements styled interactive without a semantic tag).
      const target = event.target as Element | null;
      let interactive = false;
      if (target) {
        interactive = !!target.closest(INTERACTIVE_SELECTOR);
        if (!interactive && target instanceof Element) {
          interactive = getComputedStyle(target).cursor === 'pointer';
        }
      }
      // Hero red giant: its rendered SURFACE is interactive too. The cursor can't
      // import three.js / the scene, so the live hero (HeroIsland) publishes a
      // beat-gated sphere hit-test on window.__bhHitGiant; OR it in only when not
      // already interactive (so the cheap DOM checks win first). The hook is absent
      // on pages without the scrollable hero, so the typeof guard no-ops there.
      if (!interactive) {
        const hit = (window as unknown as { __bhHitGiant?: (x: number, y: number) => boolean }).__bhHitGiant;
        if (typeof hit === 'function' && hit(targetX, targetY)) interactive = true;
      }
      cursor.classList.toggle('cursor--interactive', interactive);

      // SNAPPY BALL: pin the dot to the raw pointer SYNCHRONOUSLY, on the mousemove
      // event itself — not on the next rAF. This kills the ~16ms one-frame lag the
      // ball used to have when its transform was only written inside loop(). We are
      // safely PAST the dock early-return above (syncDock() returned false here), so
      // this write is for the NORMAL / interactive path only and can never fight the
      // loop's dock glide (which writes renderX/Y, not targetX/Y). It also covers
      // reduced motion, where the loop never runs at all.
      cursor.style.transform = `translate3d(${targetX}px, ${targetY}px, 0)`;

      // Then (re)start the loop so the comet tail animates and idles when it
      // settles — except under reduced motion, where there is no trail / loop.
      if (!reduceMotion) {
        kick();
      }
    };

    // Hide when the pointer leaves the window, restore when it returns — so the
    // ball doesn't hang frozen at the last edge position. On leave, empty the
    // ring and kick one frame so the canvas clears then parks.
    const onLeave = (): void => {
      cursor.style.opacity = '0';
      if (canvas) canvas.style.opacity = '0';
      head = 0;
      count = 0;
      // The pointer left the window: block docking and let syncDock undock so the
      // marker's own gold hex returns (the cursor that replaced it is now hidden).
      pointerInside = false;
      syncDock();
      kick();
    };
    const onEnter = (): void => {
      pointerInside = true;
      if (revealed) {
        cursor.style.opacity = '1';
        if (canvas) canvas.style.opacity = '1';
      }
    };

    // Restart the loop when the tab becomes visible again (it self-parks on hide).
    const onVisibility = (): void => {
      if (!document.hidden) kick();
    };

    // Debounced resize: re-size the backing store, then kick a redraw.
    let resizeTimer = 0;
    const onResize = (): void => {
      window.clearTimeout(resizeTimer);
      resizeTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
        resizeCanvas();
        kick();
      }, 150);
    };

    resizeCanvas();

    document.addEventListener('mousemove', onMove, { passive: true });
    document.addEventListener('mouseleave', onLeave);
    document.addEventListener('mouseenter', onEnter);
    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);
    window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);

    // Clean up on page hide (covers bfcache / navigation away). Belt-and-braces
    // for an MPA, but cheap and correct.
    window.addEventListener(
      'pagehide',
      () => {
        if (raf) cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
        raf = 0;
        window.clearTimeout(resizeTimer);
        // Drop the dock flag so a bfcache restore never starts with a stale
        // [data-cursor-docked] hiding the next page's marker gold hex.
        delete document.documentElement.dataset.cursorDocked;
        document.removeEventListener('mousemove', onMove);
        document.removeEventListener('mouseleave', onLeave);
        document.removeEventListener('mouseenter', onEnter);
        document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);
        window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
      },
      { once: true },
    );
  })();
</script>
